at first glance it seems simple to tell  one type of butterfly from the next  they're boldly colored wings appear to  differentiate one species from another  but predators must beware the wings of  butterflies tell lies  some butterflies have evolved wings that  resemble those of more poisonous species  a clever deception in other cases two  equally poisonous species have evolved  together to become mirror images of each  other the Amazonian butterfly he laconia  snoo mata has evolved in this way this  one species has many different wing  patterns copied from several different  species from the millennia genus this  kind of shared evolution is called  mullerian mimicry together Heliconius  and Melanie are spread an important  message to would-be predators don't eat  us we taste bad their similar appearance  makes them part of a cooperative that  protects them from being eaten while  Sciences new mullerian mimicry was at  work in these butterflies it was unclear  how he Laconia could derive so many  different wing patterns from only one  set of genes in the mid-1970s scientists  proposed that the wing mimicry of  Heliconius was under the control of  something called a super gene a long  stretch of DNA that contains many  smaller genes in the super gene  individual genes are immune to the  random mixing and matching that normally  occurs in sexual recombination this is  how Heliconius manages to keep its  various wing patterns separate although  the concept of a super gene fits with  what scientists observed in the field no  one has ever seen how it works until now  a study of Heliconius DNA led by french  scientist matias urine shows that the  super gene breaks and then recombines  into several different configurations  each arrangement creates a specific wing  pattern the mirror image of a melon air  species by mimicking it's poisonous  cousins Heliconius is safer from the  greedy gaze of predators so natural  selection favors these preset patterns  and by extension the genes that create  them  you  Oh
 

